        "story-1": {
          "type": "story_row",
          "settings": {
            "eyebrow": "how it started",
            "heading": "One Small Classroom, One Big Idea",
            "body": "<p>The sky can be bubblegum colored and horses can be bluish-orangey-green. For kids, imagination comes in all sorts of colors, most of which aren't in a box set.</p><p>Before she was a teacher, Tina worked in medical sales. One of the products she sold came in a little bottle with a dab-on tip. Years later, as a mom running her own preschool, she set up learning centers where kids could explore on their own, and she wanted art in them every single day. Paint made that hard. Between the cups, the brushes, and the cleanup, it just didn't come out every day.</p><p>So Tina called the company she used to work for and asked for some of those bottles. She had an idea. They became the first dot markers, and suddenly her preschoolers could dot, layer, and blend color all by themselves.</p><p>Before long, parents were asking about the waterfall of artwork coming home every day. Then they asked if they could buy a set of their own.</p>",
            "pullquote": "We help kids create their own colors, so they can express themselves freely.",
            "pullquote_attribution": "the Do-A-Dot Art! story",
            "reverse": false
          }
        },
        "story-2": {
          "type": "story_row",
          "settings": {
            "eyebrow": "how it's going",
            "heading": "Same family. Same idea. A bigger craft closet.",
            "body": "<p>Tina told her husband Rob she thought she might have a winner. A few years later she took the leap, and Do-A-Dot Art! grew from one classroom into preschools, kindergartens, and homes across the country. Their daughter Tiffany grew up in it, packing boxes in the garage when she was 12.</p><p>Today Tiffany and her husband Jesse run the business as the second generation, and Tina is still the head honcho. Parents who dotted their way through preschool are now buying dot markers for their own kids.</p><p>Our dot markers and activity books are still made in our family's factory in Montana. Our newer tools, like beeswax crayons and watercolors, are made overseas so they stay affordable for families, with the same layered learning in every one. And our paint sets are assembled with love and care by a dedicated team at a special-needs work center.</p>",
            "pullquote": "Together, let's add more color to our world.",
            "pullquote_attribution": "the Do-A-Dot Art! family",
            "reverse": true
          }
        },
